    {
      "math_academy_id": "q-120485",
      "knowledge_point_id": "a9a3d8db-a6e0-5c96-9c75-415d4a75cf4d",
      "knowledge_point": "Identifying Equations With No Solutions",
      "problem": "Which of the following equations has no solutions for ${0}^{∘}\\le x<{360}^{∘}?$\n\nI. $\\operatorname{sec}⁡x=7$ $$\n\nII. $\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{sec}⁡x+2=2\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{sec}⁡x$ $$\n\nIII. $7\\operatorname{sec}⁡x=-5$",
      "worked_solution": "We first simplify the given equations, and get\n\nI. $\\operatorname{sec}⁡x=7$ $$\n\nII. $\\operatorname{sec}⁡x=\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{3}}$\n\nIII. $\\operatorname{sec}⁡x=-\\frac{5}{7}$\n\nNow, recall that the range of $\\operatorname{sec}⁡x$ for ${0}^{∘}\\le x<{360}^{∘}$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sec}⁡x\\in (-\\infty ,-1]\\cup [1,\\infty ).\n$$\n\nSo, the equation $\\operatorname{sec}⁡x=k$ has no solutions for $k\\in (-1,1).$ \n\nTherefore, of the given equations, only equation III has no solutions.",
